          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The notion of
            %em abelian category
            is an abstraction of basic properties of the category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab"} Ab
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+groups"} abelian groups
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mod"} Mod
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
            over some
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaves"} sheaves
            of abelian groups and of modules. It is such that much of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complexes"} chain complexes
            can be developed inside every abelian category.
          %p
            The concept of abelian categories is one in a sequence of notions of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+and+abelian+categories"} additive and abelian categories
          %p
            While additive categories differ significantly from
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/toposes"} toposes
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AT+category"} AT category
            for more on that.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Recall the following fact about
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pre-abelian+categories"} pre-abelian categories
            from
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "pre-abelian+category#DecompositionOfMorphisms"} this proposition
          #DecompositionOfMorphisms.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              Every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon A\to B
              in a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pre-abelian+category"} pre-abelian category
              has a canonical de-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
            #eq:PreImageFactorization.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mover
                      %mo ↠
                      %mrow
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi p
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} coker
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                    %mi ker
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mover
                          %mi f
                          %mo ¯
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi ker
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} coker
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo ↪
                      %mrow
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi i
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    A
                    \\overset{\; p \;}{\twoheadrightarrow}
                    \\coker\big(\ker f\big)
                    \\xrightarrow{\; \overline{f} \;}
                    \\ker\big(\coker f\big)
                    \\xhookrightarrow{\; i \;}
                    B
            %p where:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
                  is a
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cokernel"} cokernel
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epimorphism
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
                  is a
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernel"} kernel
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphism"} monomorphism
          #AbelianCategory.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              An
              %strong abelian category
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pre-abelian+category"} pre-abelian category
              satisfying the following equivalent conditions.
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  For every
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi f
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mi f
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi coker
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi ker
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %mi ker
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi coker
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar{f} \colon coker(ker(f)) \to ker(coker(f))
                  %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:PreImageFactorization"} (1)
                  from prop.
                  %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DecompositionOfMorphisms"}
                  is an
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
                  (hence providing an
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/image"} image
                  factorization
                  = succeed ")." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi A
                          %mo ↠
                          %mi im
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi f
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ↪
                          %mi B
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \twoheadrightarrow im(f) \hookrightarrow B
              %li
                %p
                  Every
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphism"} monomorphism
                  is a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernel"} kernel
                  and every
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epimorphism
                  is a
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cokernel"} cokernel
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p These two conditions are indeed equivalent.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              The first condition implies that if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphism"} monomorphism
              then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi ker
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} coker
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \cong \ker(\coker(f))
              (in the category of objects over
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is a kernel. Dually if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epimorphism
              it follows that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo ≅
                      %mi coker
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ker
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \cong coker(ker(f))
            %p
              The converse can be found in, among other places, Chapter VIII of (
              %a{:href => "#MacLane"}> MacLane
              ).
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#PropertiesGeneral General
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              The notion of abelian category is self-dual:
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite
              of any abelian category is abelian.
          #RegularEpisAndMonos.num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              By the second formulation of the definition
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#AbelianCategory"}
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  every
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphism"} monomorphism
                  is a
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+monomorphism"} regular monomorphism
              %li
                %p
                  every
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epimorphism
                  is a
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+epimorphism"} regular epimorphism
            %p
              It follows that every abelian category is a
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/balanced+category"} balanced category
          %p
          #PullbackPreservesEpimorphisms.num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              In an
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
              preserves
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphisms"} epimorphisms
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pushout"} pushout
              preserves
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphisms"} monomorphisms
          Because every abelian category is a
          = succeed "." do
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+category"} regular category
          = succeed "." do
            %a{:href => "#Selick"} Selick, Prop. 1.3.13
          %h3#FactorizationOfMorphisms Factorization of morphisms
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              In an abelian category every morphism decomposes
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+the"} uniquely up to a unique isomorphism
              into the composition of an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epimorphism
              and a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphism"} monomorphism
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DecompositionOfMorphisms"}
              combined with def.
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#AbelianCategory"}
            %p
              Since by remark
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#RegularEpisAndMonos"}
              every monic is
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+monomorphism"} regular
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+monomorphism"} strong
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi epi
                    %mo ,
                    %mi mono
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (epi, mono)
              is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthogonal+factorization+system"} orthogonal factorization system
              in an abelian category; see at
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28epi%2C+mono%29+factorization+system"} (epi, mono) factorization system
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_3 Remark
            %p
              Some references claim that this property characterizes abelian categories among pre-abelian ones, but it is not clear to the authors of this page why this should be so, although we do not currently have a counterexample; see
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://nforum.mathforge.org/discussion/4094/?Focus=33415#Comment_33415"} this discussion
          %h3#CanonicalAbEnrichment
            Canonical
            = succeed "-enrichment" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ab
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ab
          %p
            The
            = succeed "-enrichment" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ab
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ab
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+small+category"} locally small
            category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            has a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zero+object"} zero object
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+monomorphism"} normal
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is abelian with respect to this structure. However, in most examples, the
            = succeed "-enrichment" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ab
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ab
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+categories"} additive categories
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoids"} monoids
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semiadditive+category"} semiadditive category
          %p
            The last point is of relevance in particular for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-category"} higher categorical
            generalizations of additive categories. See for instance
            %a{:href => "http://www.math.harvard.edu/~lurie/papers/DAG-I.pdf#page=5"} remark 2.14, p. 5
            of
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"} Jacob Lurie
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stable+Infinity-Categories"} Stable Infinity-Categories
          %h3#RelationToToposes Relation to exactness properties of toposes
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exactness+properties"} exactness properties
            of abelian categories have many features in common with exactness properties of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/toposes"} toposes
            or of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pretoposes"} pretoposes
          %p
            %a{:href => "AT+category#Freyd99"} Freyd (1999)
            gave a sharp description of the properties shared by these categories, introducing a new concept called
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AT+categories"} AT categories
            (for “abelian-topos”), and showing convincingly that the difference between the A and the T can be concentrated precisely in the difference of the behavior of the initial object.
          %h3#EmbeddingTheorems Embedding theorems
          %p
            Not every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+category"} concrete category
            such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab"} Ab
            or
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mod"} Mod
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
            it is very convenient to have a concrete abelian category, for that allows one to check the behaviour of morphisms on actual
            %em elements
            of the sets underlying the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
          %p
            The following
            = succeed "," do
              %em embedding theorems
            %em embedded
            into
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab"} Ab
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
            by an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+functor"} exact functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mi Mod
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R Mod
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Freyd-Mitchell+embedding+theorem"} Freyd-Mitchell embedding theorem
            discussed
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#FreydMitchellEmbedding"} below
          %p
            This implies for instance that proofs about
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+sequence"} exactness of sequences
            in an abelian category can always be obtained by a naive argument on elements – called a “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram+chasing"}> diagram chase
            ” – because that does hold true after such an embedding, and the exactness of the embedding means that the notion of exact sequences is preserved by it.
          %p
            Alternatively, one can reason with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+elements"} generalized elements
            in an abelian category, without explicitly embedding it into a larger concrete category, see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/element+in+an+abelian+category"} element in an abelian category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ab
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ab
            %em
              %a{:href => "#EmbeddingIntoAb"} Embedding into Ab
            below.
          %h4#counterexamples Counterexamples
          %p
            First of all, it’s easy to see that not every abelian category is equivalent to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mod"} Mod
            for some ring
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mi Mod
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R Mod
            has all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Noetherian+ring"} Noetherian ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitely+generated+module"} finitely generated
            = succeed "-modules" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
          %h4#EmbeddingIntoAb
            Embedding into
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ab
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ab
          %p (…)
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Bergman"}> Bergman 1974
            )
          %p (…)
          %h4#FreydMitchellEmbedding
            Freyd-Mitchell embedding into
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mi Mod
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R Mod
          .num_thm
            %h6#mitchells_embedding_theorem Mitchell’s Embedding Theorem
            %p
              Every small abelian category admits a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+functor"} full
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/faithful+functor"} faithful
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+functor"} exact
              functor to the category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mi Mod
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R Mod
              for some ring
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              This result can be found as Theorem 7.34 on page 150 of Peter Freyd’s book
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://www.emis.de/journals/TAC/reprints/articles/3/tr3.pdf#page=176"} Abelian Categories
              = succeed "-modules." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mitchell%27s_embedding_theorem"} Wikipedia article
              for the idea of the proof.
          %p
            For more see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Freyd-Mitchell+embedding+theorem"} Freyd-Mitchell embedding theorem
          %p
            We can also characterize which abelian categories
            %em are
            equivalent to a category of
            = succeed "-modules:" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              be an abelian category. If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              has all
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproducts"} coproducts
              and has a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+object"} compact
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+object"} projective
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generator"} generator
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi R
                    %mi Mod
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \simeq R Mod
              for some ring
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo =
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R = C(x,x)^{op}
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              is any compact projective generator. Conversely, if
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo ≃
                      %mi R
                      %mi Mod
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \simeq R Mod
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              has all small coproducts and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x = R
              is a compact projective generator.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_3 Proof
            %p
              This theorem, minus the explicit description of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://www.emis.de/journals/TAC/reprints/articles/3/tr3.pdf#page=132"} Abelian Categories
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/PS_cache/math/pdf/0506/0506603v1.pdf#page=4"} Lectures on noncommutative geometry
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              is a compact projective generator of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                      %mi Mod
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R Mod
          %p
            One can characterize functors between categories of
            = succeed "-modules" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi R
                  %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \otimes_R -
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            is a bimodule or those which look as Hom-modules. For the characterization of the tensoring functors see
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-Watts+theorem"} Eilenberg-Watts theorem
          %p Going still further one should be able to obtain a nice theorem describing the image of the embedding of the weak 2-category of
          %ul
            %li rings
            %li bimodules
            %li bimodule homomorphisms
          %p into the strict 2-category of
          %ul
            %li abelian categories
            %li right exact functors
            %li natural transformations.
          %p
            For more discussion see the
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2007/08/questions_about_modules.html"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                \-Cafe
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Of course,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab"} Ab
                is abelian,
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+modules"}
                  category
                  = succeed "Mod" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi R
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                over any
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                is abelian
            %li
              %p
                Therefore in particular the category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vect"} Vect
                of vector spaces over any field is an abelian category
            %li
              %p
                The full subcategory of
                = succeed "Mod" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                = succeed "-modules" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %li
              %p
                Similarly, the full subcategory of
                = succeed "Mod" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                = succeed "-modules" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %li
              %p
                Also similarly, the full subcategory of
                = succeed "Mod" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %li
              %p
                as is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+representations"} category of representations
                of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
                (e.g.
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://unapologetic.wordpress.com/2008/12/15/the-category-of-representations-is-abelian/"} here
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sheaves"} category of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaves+of+abelian+groups"} sheaves of abelian groups
                on any
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
                is abelian.
          %p Counter-examples:
          %ul
            %li
              The category of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsion+subgroup"} torsion-free
              abelian groups is pre-abelian, but not abelian: the monomorphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %mo :
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2:\mathbb{Z}\to\mathbb{Z}
              is not a kernel.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+and+abelian+categories"} additive and abelian categories
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+subcategory"} abelian subcategory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne+tensor+product+of+abelian+categories"} Deligne tensor product of abelian categories
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-abelian+category"} pseudo-abelian category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-abelian+category"} quasi-abelian category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semi-abelian+category"} semi-abelian category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/length+of+an+object"} length of an object
          %h2#references References
          %p
            Maybe the first reference on abelian categories, then still called
            %em exact categories
            is
          %ul
            %li
              D. A. Buchsbaum,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Exact categories and duality
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.jstor.org/stable/1993003"} JSTOR
          %p Further foundations of the theory were then laid in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Grothendieck"}> Alexander Grothendieck
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tohoku"}> Sur quelques points d'algèbre homologique
                , Tôhoku Math. J. vol 9, n.2, 3, 1957.
          %p Other classic references:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pierre+Gabriel"}> Pierre Gabriel
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Des+Cat%C3%A9gories+Ab%C3%A9liennes"} Des Catégories Abéliennes
                %strong 90
                (1962) 323-448 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item?id=BSMF_1962__90__323_0"}> numdam:BSMF_1962__90__323_0
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#Freyd64
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Freyd"}> Peter Freyd
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Abelian Categories – An Introduction to the theory of functors
                %strong 3
                (2003) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://www.emis.de/journals/TAC/reprints/articles/3/tr3abs.html"}> tac:tr3
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.emis.de/journals/TAC/reprints/articles/3/tr3.pdf"} pdf
          %p Textbook accounts:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nicolae+Popescu"}> Nicolae Popescu
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Abelian+categories+with+applications+to+rings+and+modules"} Abelian categories with applications to rings and modules
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 3
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=0340375"} MR0340375
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Saunders+MacLane"}> Saunders MacLane
                , Chapter VIII of:
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Categories+for+the+Working+Mathematician"} Categories for the Working Mathematician
                (1978)
            %li
              %p
                A. J. Berrick and M. E. Keating,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Categories and Modules, with K-theory in View
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 67
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Masaki+Kashiwara"}> Masaki Kashiwara
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pierre+Schapira"} Pierre Schapira
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Categories+and+Sheaves"} Categories and Sheaves
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 332
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/3-540-27950-4"} doi:10.1007/3-540-27950-4
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.maths.ed.ac.uk/~v1ranick/papers/kashiwara2.pdf"} pdf
            %li#EGNO15
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pavel+Etingof"}> Pavel Etingof
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Shlomo+Gelaki"} Shlomo Gelaki
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dmitri+Nikshych"} Dmitri Nikshych
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Victor+Ostrik"} Victor Ostrik
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Tensor Categories
                %strong 205
                (2015) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://bookstore.ams.org/surv-205"}> ISBN:978-1-4704-3441-0
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www-math.mit.edu/~etingof/egnobookfinal.pdf"} pdf
          %p Further review:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Rankey Datta,
                %em An introduction to abelian categories
                (2010) (
                %a{:href => "http://www-bcf.usc.edu/~lauda/teaching/rankeya.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li#Selick
              %p
                Paul Selick,
                %em Homological Algebra Notes
                (
                %a{:href => "www.math.toronto.edu/selick/mat1352/1350notes.pdf"}> pdf
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Selick_HomologicalAlgebra.pdf", :title => "pdf"}> pdf
                )
          %p
            On
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram+chasing"} diagram chasing
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elements+in+an+abelian+category"} elements in an abelian category
          %ul
            %li#Bergman
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/George+Bergman"}> George Bergman
              ,
              %em A note on abelian categories – translating element-chasing proofs, and exact embedding in abelian groups
              (1974) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://math.berkeley.edu/~gbergman/papers/unpub/elem-chase.pdf"}> pdf
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Bergman-ElementChasing.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p
            For more discussion of the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Freyd-Mitchell+embedding+theorem"} Freyd-Mitchell embedding theorem
            see there.
          %p
            The proof that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mi Mod
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R Mod
            is an abelian category is spelled out for instance in
          %ul
            %li
              Rankeya Datta,
              %em The category of modules over a commutative ring and abelian categories
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.columbia.edu/~ums/pdf/Rankeya_R-mod_and_Abelian_Categories.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            A discussion about to which extent abelian categories are a general context for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
            is archived at nForum
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.ntnu.no/~stacey/Mathforge/nForum/comments.php?DiscussionID=2052&Focus=17680#Comment_17680"} here
          %p
            See also the
            %a{:href => "http://www.mta.ca/~cat-dist/catlist/1999/atcat"} catlist 1999 discussion
            on comparison between abelian categories and topoi (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AT+categories"}> AT categories
            ).
          %p
            Formalization of abelian categories as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalent+categories"} univalent categories
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalent+foundations+of+mathematics"} univalent foundations of mathematics
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"}> homotopy type theory
            ):
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unimath"}> unimath
              ,
              %em Abelian Categories
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://unimath.github.io/doc/UniMath/4dd5c17/UniMath.CategoryTheory.Abelian.html"}> UniMath.CategoryTheory.Abelian
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            Generalization of abelian categories to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28n%2C1%29-categories"} (n,1)-categories
            is in:
          %ul
            %li
              Nao Mochizuki,
              %em Higher-dimensional generalization of abelian categories via DG Categories
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.06955"}> arXiv:2501.06955
              \&amp;rbrack;
        .revisedby
          %p
